window.docContents[151].resolve({"/part-3-internal/ch11-internal-sets/#ch11-internal-sets":{"contents":"In Ch11_InternalSets.lean (2,245 lines), a subset X \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^* is internal (IsInternal X) iff there exists a sequence of standard sets A : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathrm{Set}\\;\\mathbb{R} such that\n\n[r] \\in [A_n] \\iff \\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} : r_n \\in A_n\\} \\in \\mathcal{F}.\n\nConstant sequences A_n = A give the standard sets {}^*A, whereas varying sequences such as A_n = [0, 1/(n+1)] produce genuinely new internal subsets like the infinitesimal closed interval [0, \\varepsilon]. Transfer of Dedekind completeness now says: every nonempty bounded-above internal subset of \\mathbb{R}^* has a least upper bound in \\mathbb{R}^*! Flipping that theorem on its head immediately yields:\n\n1. External Sets: \\mathbb{I}, \\mathbb{L}, \\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{N}, and \\mathbb{N}^*_\\infty are all external because they are bounded without suprema/infima.2. Overflow & Underflow:* Overflow: If an internal set A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}^* contains arbitrarily large finite naturals (or contains all of \\mathbb{N}), then A contains an unlimited hypernatural K \\in \\mathbb{N}^*_\\infty!* Underflow: If an internal set A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}^* contains every unlimited hypernatural, it must already contain \\{m \\in \\mathbb{N}^* : m \\ge k_0\\} for some finite k_0 \\in \\mathbb{N}!3. Countable (\\aleph_1-) Saturation: Any decreasing chain A_0 \\supseteq A_1 \\supseteq A_2 \\supseteq \\cdots of nonempty internal sets in the ultrapower \\mathbb{R}^* has nonempty intersection \\bigcap_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}} A_k \\neq \\emptyset! The proof is a gem of diagonalization: pick a witness x^{(k)} \\in A_k at each step and define the diagonal sequence y_n = x^{(n)}_n—for every fixed k, y_n agrees with x^{(n)}_n \\in A_k(n) on all indices n \\ge k (a cofinite set, hence in \\mathcal{F})!4. Closedness of Shadows: Using countable saturation, for any internal set X \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^*, its standard shadow image \\mathrm{sh}(X \\cap \\mathbb{L}) \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} is automatically closed in \\mathbb{R}!\n\nsection\nopen Goldblatt\n\n-- Internal sets, star sets, and the internal S-open / hyper-open characterization:\n#check @ofSeq_mem_internalSet\n#check @isInternal_starSet\n#check @isIntervalOpen_iff_isHyperOpen_of_isInternal\n#check @isSOpen_iff_forall_halo_subset_of_isInternal\nend\n\n\n","context":"Lectures on the Hyperreals — Interactive Lean 4 & Spatial Edition\u0009Part III — Internal & External Entities","header":"3.1. Chapter 11: Internal Sets, Overflow & Countable Saturation","id":"/part-3-internal/ch11-internal-sets/#ch11-internal-sets"}});